{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"The record is complete; one last precision fix — anchoring the verification account's \"documented purpose\" claim to the API comment itself."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_18-00-28-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nu={\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Small precision fix on the leader-verification account: the account leans on the documented purpose of VerifyLeader to argue the consequence, but that purpose was only quoted from reading, not anchored. The API comment and declaration are added as a source and cited, so the claim about what the call is meant to detect is anchored like the rest of the account. No execution, comparison or conclusion changes.\\\",\n \\\"sources\\\":[\n  {\\\"id\\\":\\\"s-verifyleader-doc\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"api.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":928,\\\"end_line\\\":941,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"}\n ],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[\n  {\\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"790eb5d64ed94ef5aea4736e6f66381d-question-0\\\",\n   \\\"experiment_id\\\":\\\"f6dbda4bb1ee4baca7e8f055f109412f-experiment\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-verifyleader\\\",\\\"s-replstate\\\",\\\"s-heartbeat\\\",\\\"s-lease\\\",\\\"s-quorum\\\",\\\"s-verifyleader-doc\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"7401ab5a8f7c4cd69d947e972ea31a61\\\":[22,23,24,25,26,27],\\\"c0f7723f05d8430aaf36bf2b4f9ce862\\\":[22,23,24,25],\\\"5e092a2b160b4a2f955d48ee6fa06ad9\\\":[22,23]},\n   \\\"joint_history\\\":\\\"Three receipts answer this question. The first (5e092a2b160b4a2f955d48ee6fa06ad9 at indices 22 and 23) measured the isolation with slowed timers; the second (c0f7723f05d8430aaf36bf2b4f9ce862 at 22-25) ran it with slowed and default timers; the third (7401ab5a8f7c4cd69d947e972ea31a61 at 22-27) repeats both cases and measures when the isolated leader stops reporting itself as leader.\\\",\n   \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Current account: leader verification can be satisfied without a voter quorum, and the resulting incorrect answer is available for about one lease interval before the same node stops leading. Evidence: in the latest receipt the slowed-timer setup event 22 records three voters with a quorum of two, a voter unreachable by direct AppendEntries and a reachable non-voter, and its result event 23 records verification succeeding with no error in under a millisecond while the node still led; events 25 and 26 repeat the premise and the same result with the repository's default in-memory timers; the bound events 24 and 27 record the node becoming a follower after 1012 ms with a 1000 ms lease and after 60 ms with the default 50 ms lease. The earlier receipts show the same two verification results, so the finding is reproduced across three runs. Mechanism from source: verifyLeader starts its count at one for itself and registers the future in every entry of the leader's replication map, which startStopReplication builds for every server of the configuration except the leader without filtering by suffrage; each peer's heartbeat acknowledges the pending verification on a successful AppendEntries, so a non-voter's acknowledgement raises the count to the voter quorum computed from voters alone, while the lease check counts voters only and therefore closes the window about one lease interval later. The documented purpose of the call, now anchored, is to ensure the peer is still the leader and to prevent returning stale data from the FSM after leadership is lost, which is exactly the situation the non-voter acknowledgement cannot establish.\\\",\n   \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"violated\\\",\n   \\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"asserted\\\",\n   \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\n    \\\"The measured bound limits the impact: the node stops reporting itself as leader about one lease interval after losing the voter quorum, so the incorrect verification result is available only inside that interval.\\\",\n    \\\"The defect requires a configuration containing non-voters; in a voters-only cluster every replication peer is a voter and the acknowledgement set equals the voter set, so the path cannot diverge from the voter quorum.\\\",\n    \\\"No write path is affected: entry commitment uses the commitment map, which contains voters only, so this is a read-verification path rather than a commit-rule path.\\\",\n    \\\"The probe calls VerifyLeader directly rather than through an application that then serves a read, so the stale-read consequence is argued from the anchored documentation rather than measured end to end.\\\"\n   ],\n   \\\"limitations\\\":[\n    \\\"Measured on four-peer in-memory clusters with the repository's own facilities; production timings and transports are not covered.\\\",\n    \\\"The bound is reported for the two timer configurations used (1000 ms and the default 50 ms lease) and depends on the lease timeout, not on a fixed wall-clock constant.\\\",\n    \\\"Both cases isolate the leader by transport disconnection and confirm it with direct AppendEntries attempts recorded in the same events.\\\"\n   ]}\n ]\n}\njson.dump(u,open('update.json','w'),indent=2); print('written')\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_18-00-28-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update.json 2>&1 | tail -2\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
